Module hessel "The Banns at Hessel" {
  // ── The morning after the Ball ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // At nine on the fifth morning Mrs Cherrett, if she woke, sends for her legs; if she did not, her will does. Either
  // way the company is paid. What it is given is Josiah Cherrett's compass, which found him the line of three canals.
  Kind cherrett_compass "Josiah Cherrett's pocket compass" like old_glossary granting wayfinding worth 5 weighing 0 described "A brass pocket compass in a case worn smooth by a navvy's thumb, with J.C. scratched inside the lid and, under it, smaller, A. He found the line of three canals with it.".
  Item compass "Josiah Cherrett's pocket compass" is cherrett_compass at limbo.
  Relation thanked(group).
  Rule thanks_living at cherrett_lodging when fifthDay() and hour(clock) >= 9 and not gone(cherrett) and legs(party) and not thanked(party) and (at(party, cherrett_parlour) or at(party, cherrett_bedroom))
    says "Mrs Cherrett is up, and dressed, and packing, which is to say that she is sitting in the middle of the parlour telling three maids how to pack. She sees you and stops. 'There you are,' she says. 'My legs.' She counts twenty coins into your hand out of the pudding-cloth purse, and then, after a moment, takes something out of her bodice on a ribbon and gives you that too: a brass pocket compass, worn smooth. 'Josiah's,' she says. 'He found the line of three canals with it. He'd want it to go somewhere.' She looks at you. 'You did well, mostly. You did some things I'd have had your hide for, a week ago.' She waves her stick. 'Go on. Go and see the coaches off. I'm not crying, it's the dropsy.'" {
    Assert thanked(party). Increase quantity(party, gold) by 20. Assert at(compass, cherrett_parlour). }
  Rule thanks_will at cherrett_lodging when fifthDay() and hour(clock) >= 9 and gone(cherrett) and legs(party) and not thanked(party) and (at(party, cherrett_parlour) or at(party, circus))
    says "Mopsa meets you at the door with a paper in her hand. 'She wrote it on Saturday,' she says. 'A codicil. Her lawyer's going to have a fit.' She reads it out, in Mrs Cherrett's words, which are not a lawyer's: To my legs, who caught me, or tried to, twenty pound and Josiah's compass, as I've nobody else would know what it was for. Mopsa gives you the compass, on its ribbon, still warm from where it lay on the dressing-table. 'She'd have liked the funeral,' says Mopsa. 'She'd have laughed all through it.'" {
    Assert thanked(party). Increase quantity(party, gold) by 20. Assert at(compass, cherrett_parlour). }
  // Kit, if he is to marry an heiress, will not stay on the poles; he gives his to whoever helped.
  Kind chair_pole "an ash chair-pole, nine feet, polished by twenty years of hands" like quarterstaff worth 3 weighing 3.
  Item kit_pole "Kit Danby's chair-pole" is chair_pole at limbo.
  Relation poleGiven(group).
  Rule kit_gives_pole at stall_street when (kitEngaged(kit) or cherrettBlessesKit(cherrett)) and fifthDay() and hour(clock) >= 8 and not poleGiven(kit) and at(party, stall_street)
    says "Kit Danby is on Stall Street in his Sunday coat, not his blue one, and he holds out one of his poles to you, the near-side one, ash, polished black by twenty years of other men's hands before his. 'I'll not be carrying,' he says. 'Not for a while. Lanyon says it's a sin to sell a pole, so I'm giving it.' He puts it in your hands. 'It's a good weight for a staff. I've seen a man stop a runaway horse with one.' He grins, for the first time you have ever seen. 'Or a bath-chair.'" {
    Assert poleGiven(kit). Assert at(kit_pole, stall_street). }
  // Mopsa, engaged and without a house to keep, will go on the road with the company; Ottery, if he came home.
  Rule mopsa_willing when mopsaYes(party) and not mopsaHouse(mopsa) and not willing(mopsa, party) and not travelsWith(mopsa, party) { Assert willing(mopsa, party). }
  Initially Set hire(mopsa) to 0.

  // ── The Season's end ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  // At noon on the fifth day the coaches go, and the book closes on an account: each strand's morning after, told
  // plainly, and then one ending, by whether Mrs Cherrett woke. No custom, no ever-after; the morning, and stop.
  Query accounting() means seasonOver() and cameToHessel(party).
  Rule fate_ismay_wed when accounting() and married(couple_kit) and not spread(fate_ismay) says "Ismay Cherrett was married to Christopher Danby, chairman, at nine o'clock on a weekday at Saint Ebba's, by a licence with the names filled in afterwards, and the Season, getting into its coaches, could talk of nothing else. She keeps the books of the Stall Street stand now. They have never balanced so well." { Assert spread(fate_ismay). }
  Rule fate_ismay_engaged when accounting() and (kitEngaged(kit) or cherrettBlessesKit(cherrett)) and not married(couple_kit) and not spread(fate_ismay) says "Ismay Cherrett and Kit Danby are to be married at home in the autumn, by banns, read three times, with nobody pausing; Kit has given up the poles, and Mrs Cherrett has given him a canal to look after, which he says is just a very long chair." { Assert spread(fate_ismay). }
  Rule fate_ismay_hartopp when accounting() and hartoppClaims(ismay) and not hartoppRuined(hartopp) and not spread(fate_ismay) says "Ismay Cherrett goes home engaged to Dr Hartopp, because he stood up with her twice running at the Closing Ball and nobody stopped him, and in Hessel that is the same thing as a promise. She keeps her own accounts still. In the margin of the household book, against his name, she has written a small exact sum." { Assert spread(fate_ismay). }
  Rule fate_ismay_jilted when accounting() and ismayJilted(ismay) and not spread(fate_ismay) says "Ismay Cherrett goes home unmarried, jilted at the altar, the Season says, though it was not her that was left. She sits in the coach with her ledger on her knee and looks out of the window at Stall Street as it goes by, at the chairmen's bench, and does not turn a page." { Assert spread(fate_ismay). }
  Rule fate_ismay_free when accounting() and not spread(fate_ismay) says "Ismay Cherrett goes home unmarried, with her ledger, which is more than she came with." { Assert spread(fate_ismay). }
  Entity fate_ismay: fact "Ismay".
  Rule fate_rupert_family when accounting() and grandmother(lady_wenlock) and not spread(fate_rupert) says "Lady Wenlock has taken the lease of a house in Quay Lane for the winter, three doors from a milliner's, and is said to be learning to make bonnets, very badly. The Honourable Rupert Wenlock and his wife walk on the Beacon at noon with their son between them, in a pair, the way the Season walks it." { Assert spread(fate_rupert). }
  Rule fate_rupert_spoke when accounting() and rupertSpoke(rupert) and not grandmother(lady_wenlock) and not spread(fate_rupert) says "Rupert Wenlock stood up in Saint Ebba's and said it himself, and his mother has not spoken to him since, and he has gone home to Quay Lane with his hat on the peg, and is the happiest man in Hessel, and the poorest." { Assert spread(fate_rupert). }
  Rule fate_rupert_scandal when accounting() and scandal(wenlock) and not grandmother(lady_wenlock) and not spread(fate_rupert) says "The Wenlocks left Hessel by the first coach after the scandal, Lady Wenlock in one and her son, his wife and child in another, going different ways. Mrs Scrope has told the story so often she has begun to improve it." { Assert spread(fate_rupert). }
  Rule fate_rupert_quiet when accounting() and not spread(fate_rupert) and withdrawnByCherrett(ebbas) says "The Wenlock match was broken off by Mrs Cherrett, with regret expressed, and Lady Wenlock took it as a great insult and went home; and Rupert went down to Quay Lane every noon to the end of the Season, as before, with his secret, and nobody in Hessel any the wiser." { Assert spread(fate_rupert). }
  Rule fate_rupert_none when accounting() and not spread(fate_rupert) says "The Honourable Rupert Wenlock goes home with his mother, and his secret, and his beetle." { Assert spread(fate_rupert). }
  Entity fate_rupert: fact "Rupert".
  Rule fate_rob_wed when accounting() and married(couple_rob) and not spread(fate_rob) says "Rob Cherrett married Celia Carth at nine in the morning at Saint Ebba's, by licence, with the names filled in; she wore no fur cap. He has written a poem since that is very nearly good." { Assert spread(fate_rob). }
  Rule fate_rob_celia when accounting() and robCeliaSpoke(rob) and celiaOut(celia) and not married(couple_rob) and not spread(fate_rob) says "Rob Cherrett is not engaged to Miss Pinnock, or to anybody, yet. He is staying on in Hessel after the Season, lodging in the Crescent, three doors from Carth House, where a girl reads Ovid in the window with the shutter open." { Assert spread(fate_rob). }
  Rule fate_rob_window when accounting() and celiaHasPoem(celia) and not celiaOut(celia) and not spread(fate_rob) says "Rob Cherrett goes home with his aunt, and writes, and does not show anyone what he writes. In the end house of the Crescent a girl in a fur cap keeps a poem in her Ovid, to mark the place." { Assert spread(fate_rob). }
  Rule fate_rob_none when accounting() and not spread(fate_rob) says "Rob Cherrett goes home unengaged, with eleven bad poems, and looks back at the Crescent from the coach until it is gone." { Assert spread(fate_rob). }
  Entity fate_rob: fact "Rob".
  Rule fate_carth_open when accounting() and carthReunited(carth) and not spread(fate_carth) says "The shutters of Carth House stand open the whole length of the house, and its fires are out, and its clocks are going, all of them wrong. Lady Carth does not go back to the laundries, but she goes down on Thursdays, with Brannagh, and stands at the coppers with her sleeves up, talking, and nobody stops her." { Assert spread(fate_carth). }
  Rule fate_carth_door when accounting() and doorOpenedByOther(carth) and not spread(fate_carth) says "Nobody knocks at Carth House at two in the morning any more. Sir Gervase still sits up in the hall, in his greatcoat, facing the door, with the bolts drawn, now, and the door on the latch, and nobody comes." { Assert spread(fate_carth). }
  Rule fate_carth_fled when accounting() and nanFled(nan) and not doorOpenedByOther(carth) and not spread(fate_carth) says "Nan Bellamy left the laundries the day the town came down to look at her, and nobody knows where she went. At Carth House the fires burn and the clocks say two." { Assert spread(fate_carth). }
  Rule fate_carth_celia when accounting() and celiaTaken(celia) and not carthReunited(carth) and not spread(fate_carth) says "Celia Carth is out, and does not go back. Sir Gervase sits in his hall with the fires burning and the clocks at two, and the knock still comes at two, and now there is nobody upstairs either." { Assert spread(fate_carth). }
  Rule fate_carth_none when accounting() and not spread(fate_carth) says "At the end of the Crescent the shutters of Carth House are closed and its chimneys smoke, and tonight at two someone will come up the Parade in a grey cloak and knock once." { Assert spread(fate_carth). }
  Entity fate_carth: fact "the Carths".
  Rule fate_ottery_road when accounting() and travelsWith(ottery, party) and not spread(fate_ottery) says "Dr Ottery is on the road with you, with his bag, arguing." { Assert spread(fate_ottery). }
  Rule fate_ottery_licensed when accounting() and otteryLicensed(ottery) and not spread(fate_ottery) says "There is a twelfth brass plate on Doctors' Walk, very small and badly polished: J. OTTERY, PHYSICIAN, WHO WILL TELL YOU. He has more patients than he wants, and charges the rich double so as to see the poor for nothing." { Assert spread(fate_ottery). }
  Rule fate_ottery_gone when accounting() and otteryGone(ottery) and not spread(fate_ottery) says "Dr Ottery went north from the gate of Orm, and the Hospital of the Waters has a new physician, recommended by Dr Hartopp, who is very pleasant." { Assert spread(fate_ottery). }
  Rule fate_ottery_struck when accounting() and otteryStruck(ottery) and not spread(fate_ottery) says "Dr Ottery is struck off the College's roll, and physicks the Old Town without a licence for a shilling a time, or a pie, and the College at Orm has decided not to know." { Assert spread(fate_ottery). }
  Rule fate_ottery_none when accounting() and not spread(fate_ottery) says "Dr Ottery packed his bag and went, and nobody in Hessel asked where." { Assert spread(fate_ottery). }
  Entity fate_ottery: fact "Ottery".
  Rule fate_mopsa_wed when accounting() and married(couple_mopsa) and not spread(fate_mopsa) says "Mopsa Gedge married you at nine o'clock at Saint Ebba's by a licence with the names filled in afterwards, and did not change her name, and laughed in the vestry until Mr Pyne had to sit down." { Assert spread(fate_mopsa). }
  Rule fate_mopsa_both when accounting() and mopsaYes(party) and mopsaHouse(mopsa) and not married(couple_mopsa) and not spread(fate_mopsa) says "Mopsa Gedge has a house in Quay Lane and an understanding with you, on her terms, both of which she mentions to everyone, in that order." { Assert spread(fate_mopsa). }
  Rule fate_mopsa_house when accounting() and mopsaHouse(mopsa) and not spread(fate_mopsa) says "Mopsa Gedge has the lease of Number Four, Quay Lane, and a card in the window: ROOMS, RESPECTABLE, NO PHYSICIANS. Her first lodger is a chairman's widow; her second, Mr Pyne." { Assert spread(fate_mopsa). }
  Rule fate_mopsa_yes when accounting() and mopsaYes(party) and not spread(fate_mopsa) says "Mopsa Gedge has said yes to you, on her terms, and has said nothing else about it to anybody, which is how you know she means it." { Assert spread(fate_mopsa). }
  Rule fate_mopsa_none when accounting() and not spread(fate_mopsa) says "Mopsa Gedge goes home in the second coach with her book and Mrs Cherrett's shawl, a companion still, and looks out of the window at Quay Lane as it goes by." { Assert spread(fate_mopsa). }
  Entity fate_mopsa: fact "Mopsa".
  Rule fate_chairmen when accounting() and faresRestored(chairmen) and not spread(fate_chairs) says "The fares stand at ninepence the Parade, and Mr Twiss's speech to the Corporation is printed as a broadside and sold on Stall Street at a penny, to the chairmen, who frame it." { Assert spread(fate_chairs). }
  Rule fate_chairmen_struck when accounting() and stoodWith(party) and not faresRestored(chairmen) and not spread(fate_chairs) says "The chairmen kept the poles crossways to the end of the Season, and the Season walked; and the Corporation, which had to walk too, restored the fares in a week." { Assert spread(fate_chairs). }
  Rule fate_chairmen_scab when accounting() and scab(party) and not spread(fate_chairs) says "On Stall Street nobody in a blue coat will say your name." { Assert spread(fate_chairs). }
  Entity fate_chairs: fact "the chairmen".
  Rule fate_hartopp_ruined when accounting() and (hartoppRuined(hartopp) or hartoppBarred(hartopp) and hartoppDismissed(hartopp)) and not spread(fate_hartopp) says "Dr Hartopp has taken his plate down from Doctors' Walk and gone to practise in a town where the waters are colder and the patients do not talk to each other." { Assert spread(fate_hartopp). }
  Rule fate_hartopp_barred when accounting() and (hartoppBarred(hartopp) or hartoppCooled(hartopp)) and not spread(fate_hartopp) says "Dr Hartopp is not seen at the Rooms, and his practice has fallen away by half, and he is very pleasant to everyone who is left." { Assert spread(fate_hartopp). }
  Rule fate_hartopp_well when accounting() and not spread(fate_hartopp) says "Dr Hartopp has eleven new patients for next Season and a hundred pounds in prospect, and prescribes hope, which he never charges for." { Assert spread(fate_hartopp). }
  Entity fate_hartopp: fact "Hartopp".

  // ── The endings ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  Ending turned_away "Turned Away" when accounting() and legsLost(party) "The coaches go at noon from the Abbey Yard, and you watch them from the Pump Room colonnade, where nobody looks at you. Mrs Cherrett's goes past with the blinds half down. In the second, Mopsa Gedge sees you, and looks at you for a long moment, and does not wave. The steam stands up off the Baths, and the bells of Saint Ebba's ring for nothing in particular, and the Season is over, and you were in it, and it was not yours.".
  Ending seasons_end "The Season's End" when accounting() and gone(cherrett) "Mrs Cherrett did not wake on the last morning of the Season, and the coaches went without her at noon from the Abbey Yard, and her own went last, empty but for Mopsa, Ismay and Rob, and her stick, which nobody could bring themselves to put in the boot. They bury her at home, by Josiah, under a stone she wrote herself: ABIGAIL CHERRETT, WHO COOKED FOR THE DELVE CUT. It says nothing about the forty thousand pounds. Everybody who was at the Rooms that Season remembers the laugh, and nobody remembers why she was barred.".
  Ending last_dance "The Last Dance" when accounting() and cherrettDanced(cherrett) "At noon the coaches go from the Abbey Yard, and the last of them is Mrs Cherrett's, with her in it, wide awake, in purple, with her stick across her knees, and the blinds up so that she can see everything. Mr Twiss is on the Pump Room steps in his dove-grey, and as the coach goes by he takes off his hat and bows the bow that is several bows, and she laughs out of the window at him so that the whole Abbey Yard hears it. She will not see another Season, and she knows it, and she is not, at this moment, minding at all.".
  Ending morning_after "The Morning After" when accounting() and legs(party) "At noon the coaches go from the Abbey Yard, one after another, down Stall Street and over Lim Bridge, and the Season goes with them: the colonels and the bishops' nieces and the widows in lavender. Mrs Cherrett's goes last, with the blinds up, and her in it, awake and cross and hungry, waving her stick at you from the window as if you were a canal gang that had stopped for its dinner. Then the Abbey Yard is empty, and the pumper is putting the chairs up on the tables, and Hessel is a town on a hill with hot water in it, until next year.".
  Ending passing_through "Passing Through" when accounting() and not legs(party) and not legsLost(party) "At noon the coaches go from the Abbey Yard, and the Season goes with them, and you watch it go from the colonnade like everybody else who was never quite in it. A very large old woman in purple goes by with the blinds up, and looks at you as if she might once have bought you, and then she is gone. The pumper puts the chairs up on the tables.".
}
